# LF everywhere, in the index AND in the working directory, on every platform. This matches the Odin
# project's own .editorconfig, which declares `end_of_line = lf` despite Odin being developed mainly on
# Windows.
#
# `text=auto` alone only guarantees LF in the *index*; what lands in the working directory is then left
# to each developer's `core.autocrlf`, which Git for Windows sets to `true` in its system config. That
# gives Windows checkouts CRLF files while .editorconfig (end_of_line = lf) and the tools that rewrite
# files in place - odinfmt above all - emit LF, so a formatting pass leaves a file with mixed endings
# and Git reports "<line-endings changed since checkout>". Adding `eol=lf` pins the working directory
# too and overrides core.autocrlf, so the whole toolchain agrees. Odin, just, nu and Sublime all read
# LF happily on Windows.
#
# Keep this in sync with .editorconfig's `end_of_line` and odinfmt.json's `newline_style`.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts are the exception: some interpreters need CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Never touch the contents of these. Everything here is gitignored, so this only matters for a file
# committed deliberately or force-added - but that is exactly the case where `* text=auto eol=lf`
# above would otherwise corrupt it. Kept in step with .gitignore's build-artifact list, which spells
# out which extension belongs to which platform.
*.exe binary
*.pdb binary
*.lib binary
*.dll binary
*.obj binary
*.o binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.a binary
*.bc binary
*.spall binary

# Editor configuration, not project source. Linguist reads `.sublime-build` / `.sublime-project` as
# JSON with Comments and `.sublime-snippet` as XML, so in a repository this small they visibly skew
# the language bar away from the Odin the project is actually written in. `vendored` rather than
# `generated`: these are hand-written, they just are not the project's own code - which stays true
# after `odin-skel new` copies them, so this rule survives into a scaffolded project.
#
# The trailing `**` is load-bearing. gitattributes borrows gitignore's pattern syntax but not its
# directory semantics: a pattern matching a directory does NOT recursively cover the paths inside it,
# so a bare `.sublime/` would apply to nothing at all.
.sublime/** linguist-vendored=true

# >>> skeleton-only
# Generated files: `tools/skel/templates.odin` comes from `just embed`, the two snippets from
# `just snippets`. Marking them collapses their diffs by default on GitHub and keeps them out of the
# repository's language statistics, so a regenerated listing does not bury the real change in a
# review. CI (`embed-check`, `snippets-check`) is what actually enforces they stay current.
#
# All three are true of this repository only, which is why the block is stripped from a scaffolded
# project: `tools/` is not copied at all, and while the snippets are, the `snippets` recipe that
# generates them is itself skeleton-only - so over there they are ordinary checked-in files and
# `linguist-generated` would be a false claim. They keep the `linguist-vendored` above either way,
# which is what actually keeps them out of the language statistics; this block adds the "regenerate,
# do not hand-edit" signal on top, and only where it is honest.
tools/skel/templates.odin linguist-generated=true
.sublime/Just-Odin.sublime-snippet linguist-generated=true
.sublime/Just-Odin-lib.sublime-snippet linguist-generated=true
.sublime/Odin-skeleton.sublime-snippet linguist-generated=true
# <<< skeleton-only
